  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of national and sub-national institutions to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The CPO contributes to the India DWCP 2023-27, Priority 3, OUTCOME 3.2: By 2027, fair, effective and inclusive policy frameworks, institutional mechanisms and services are developed to reduce discrimination and inequalities and to protect the rights of migrant workers, women workers, especially those in the informal economy and workers in vulnerable situations.
India is a country of origin and transit, and destination for workers crossing international borders. Historically, migration for work has been a path to prosperity for millions of Indians. Latest data indicates that India has the world&#x2019;s largest diaspora with over 35 million Non-Resident Indians (NRIs) &amp; Persons of Indian Origin (PIOs) across the globe and received US$125 billion in remittances in 2023.  The Government of India (GoI) has laid important focus on international labour migration from India and has signed Migration and Mobility Agreements with France, Germany, Austria, Italy, Australia and UK and labour mobility agreements with GCC Countries, Israel, Taiwan, Portugal. These agreements steer migration flows in country specific corridors and reflect on issues related to skills, education, recruitment, language, family reunification amongst others. India is also engaging with destination countries through high-level dialogues and regional forums like Abu Dhabi Dialogue, Colombo Process, India-EU High Level Dialogue which consider emergent issues across a range of migration subjects. To facilitate mobility of skilled workers, India is also aligning cooperation on mutual recognition of qualification and/or skills recognition through formal agreements and pilot programmes with demands from foreign labour markets.
According to Census 2011, there are over 450 million migrants in India of which over 41 million people reported work as the reason for migration. Majority of internal migrants work in the agriculture, industries, and services sectors, which also constituted a large part of the informal econo</narrative>
  </description>
